// EXPECT: PASS
// A subtle case, isolated from tests/05_reopen.adl's "f1: c" - which
// comments that this should be a *rejected*, "faulty" lookup. Given the
// stated algorithm (ascend until the first name is found in some
// ancestor's own-or-inherited children, with no exclusion of scopes
// already passed through on the way up), this should actually succeed:
// ascending from d (a.b.c.d) for a bare "c" reaches b two levels up,
// and b's own child is named "c" - which happens to be the very object
// (a.b.c) already on the ascent path. That coincidence doesn't make the
// find wrong; b's own child "c" is legitimately "in scope" once ascent
// reaches b. If this is not the intended behaviour after all, 05_reopen's
// comment was right and this file's marker should flip instead.
TOP {
	a: {
		b: {
			c:;
		}
	}
	a.b.c.d: {
		looked_up: c;
	}
}
